💡 **SUGGESTION: pin the native-adopted `status` surface — the twin of this foreign-side assert**

The new group drives `bash_job status` through the real tool only for the **foreign** side (`no captured log`, asserted here). The status behavior this PR actually changes — a native-adopted job showing `log: <path>` via `_shellJobStatusLine`'s new `entry.isAdopted && entry.logPath.isEmpty` branch — has no assertion anywhere, so its headline surface can silently regress (the AC1 test asserts `entry.logPath` and `output`, never `status`).

This file's own convention pins every branch of the adopted renderers ("every branch is unit-pinned in adopted_shell_jobs_test", `agent_cli_waiting.dart:1224`). Twin assert in the AC1 test (or here):

```dart
final status = await _toolText(bashJobTool(registry), {
  'action': 'status',
  'id': 'sh-9-prev',
});
expect(status, contains('log: $logPath'));
expect(status, isNot(contains('no captured log')));
```

